 "notes": "Digitised the black BSD-analysis curve of Fig. 3 (updated/erratum version, O3_DPDM_UL_erratum.png from the arXiv v3 e-print), which the paper states is the most constraining at most masses (headline result); the red cross-correlation curve is weaker after the erratum correction. Figure gives 95% CL upper limits on eps^2 for U(1)_B coupling vs frequency 10-2000 Hz. Converted frequency to mass via m = h*f (m[eV] = 4.1357e-15 * f[Hz]). Converted to canonical U(1)_B-L gauge coupling using the paper's statement that U(1)_B-L limits are eps^2(B-L) = 4*eps^2(B) (Fig. 3 caption), and g_BL = e*eps with e = sqrt(4*pi*alpha) = 0.3028 (coupling defined as eps*e*J^mu*A_mu in Eq. 1). So g_BL = 2*e*sqrt(eps^2_B). Extraction validated against quoted best BSD constraint eps^2 ~ 2.4e-47 at m_A ~ 5.7e-13 eV (extracted 2.55e-47 at 139 Hz = 5.75e-13 eV). Curve median-binned into 100 log-spaced points; narrow spikes smoothed. DM density assumed: rho_DM = 4e14 eV/m^3 = 0.4 GeV/cm^3.",
